tests:
  unit: { count: 21, covers: "A key maps to data-<lowercase hex of its UTF-8>, label-safe and injective (prefix keys, unicode, empty). The frame (4-byte big-endian caption length, caption JSON, bytes) round-trips with and without a type hint in Rust and TS alike, and a short, overlong or non-caption frame is refused with why (Rust) or read as nothing (TS). A parked payload is claimed exactly once, parking again under a label keeps the first, a window with nothing parked claims nothing, and only the main window's destruction takes data windows with it. In TS the shell path sends the frame as the raw body of open_data_window and reads take_data_payload's raw answer, an empty one as nothing; the browser fallback parks on the opener under the window's name, a child claims once, an open window is focused without re-parking, a closed one's key opens anew, a non-data window claims nothing." }
  integration: { count: 31, covers: "Through both commands on tauri's mock runtime: a 10 MiB payload opened from main reaches its data window byte-identical, once; two opens with one key yield one window holding the first payload; another key yields a second; main claims nothing; a body that is not a frame opens nothing; main's destruction (on_destroyed, which the window-event hook runs) destroys every data window; a destroyed data window drops only its own unclaimed payload. xtask/src/ui.rs: both shell builds register exactly the commands the app invokes (now including open_data_window and take_data_payload), and the new 'the e2e build enables every capability the app ships' holds data-window enabled in tauri.conf.e2e.json; the file's other UI guards stay green. Full vitest suite at d4c21681: 60,771 in 67 files, green; shell cargo test 157 + 7 green; clippy -D warnings green in the real and scenario builds." }
